{
  "timestamp": "2026-08-12T18:02:18Z",
  "title": "Sharing A Draft Before It Goes Live, On A Site With No Server",
  "type": "feature",
  "userRequest": "Project context: We need to modernize the https://www.harvardintech.com/ website from the outside. The site was built with a no-code product so we need to faithfully reproduce it using modern technologies and host it as a GitHub pages website. It also needs a CRM that can be run locally or, ideally also in the cloud via GitHub pages as well (protected with a password and/or GitHub OAuth). Can we dig into this?",
  "description": "Upgraded @codeyam/cms 0.4.0 to 0.5.0 and wired its preview links across the five collections with a per-entry route: pages, blog, chapters, communities and volunteer projects. An editor can now put an unpublished entry at an unguessable URL and hand it to one reviewer who has no GitHub account, no CMS access and no site passphrase — and optionally protect it with a password, which on a static host can only honestly mean the title and body are encrypted at rest and decrypt in the reader's browser.\n\nThe upgrade retired half the patch. The publish deploy watch shipped upstream in 0.5.0, so patches/@codeyam+cms+0.4.0.patch became @codeyam+cms+0.5.0.patch and shrank from 1,859 lines to 534, with deployWatch/deployStage/deployMarker tests turning from patch guards into contracts on the released dependency. The reorder arrows did NOT land upstream and were re-derived against 0.5.0's rewritten EntryRow rather than replayed onto it; the dependency is pinned exactly, because npm's ^0.5.0 would let a patch release silently drop the arrows — the failure CMS_SETUP.md was written to prevent.\n\nFour pieces make preview links work and each fails quietly alone: previewFields in the schemas (zod strips unknown keys, so without it every filter sees an ordinary page), routableEntries in getStaticPaths (build without list — publishedEntries there 404s every link), noindex from SEO.astro (the review track's gate is off on the public build, so a preview merged to main would otherwise be indexable), and a sitemap filter (sitemap.xml is public; no noindex walks back a published token). src/lib/drafts.ts keeps its positional includeDrafts signature and delegates to the package rather than adopting its options object, because the fifteen existing call sites would have type-checked and silently fallen back to the package's environment guess — discarding the two-track draft rule the review site exists for.\n\nOne deliberate hole: preview URLs are exempt from the crimson2026 passphrase (gateAppliesTo). Gating them would mean sending a reviewer the link and the site password together, handing them the whole unreleased site to read one page; the unguessable URL is the access mechanism instead, the same trade already made for the design-review directory. It is a real trade — anyone forwarded the link can read the page — and password protection is the answer when that is not acceptable.\n\nOne defect found and fixed mid-build: locked previews first rendered raw base64 at the reader. The package README documents the schema and routing wiring but not that a locked entry's stored body is ciphertext, so a route rendering it as markdown shows a wall of unreadable text. All five routes now branch through BlogPostBody, which chose to be its own component precisely so that rule is stated once next to its reason.",
